    {
      "id": "p1",
      "text": "The data showed an upward shift in border activity metrics this spring. Observers of the data noted a clear pattern emerged in the numbers: apprehensions along the U.S. border rose from 6,598 in February to 8,268 in March, a change linked to seasonal climate. Yet, this statistical trend sits within a sustained state of reduced crossing, an enforcement policy outcome of strict measures. Meanwhile, policy architects remain in a state of legislative stalemate over funding disputes for enforcement agencies, illustrating a failure of the creative process.",
